<title>FRANCE: BELGIAN RENAULT WORKERS CLASH WITH FRENCH POLICE.</title>
<headline>BELGIAN RENAULT WORKERS CLASH WITH FRENCH POLICE.</headline>
<dateline>WAVRIN, France 1997-03-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Belgian Renault workers clashed with French police on Thursday outside a compound in northern France where the automobile company has thousands of cars stored, witnesses said.</p>
<p>Para-military gendarmes protecting the site at Wavrin, south of the city of Lille, fired tear gas after being stoned by some 400 workers from the Renault assembly plant at Vilvoorde near Brussels, due to be closed in July with the loss of 3,100 jobs.</p>
<p>Police reinforcements were brought in to disperse the workers, who drove to France by bus to carry out surprise actions against Renault after chairman Louis Schweitzer told union representatives on Wednesday that the decision to close the Vilvoorde plant was irrevocable.</p>
</text>
